00:41:11.860 --> 00:41:17.060
 quantities of the pollen and nectar containing neonicotinoids. So even if they aren't directly

00:41:17.060 --> 00:41:23.380
 sprayed, right, they could experience the sublethal effects that I described. And so that's part of why

00:41:23.380 --> 00:41:29.910
 they're so, why people are so concerned about them, why there's a moratorium on them in Europe,

00:41:29.910 --> 00:41:36.630
 why the state of Maryland just banned them for, not for farming, but for ornamental use,

00:41:36.630 --> 00:41:40.150
 and why California is also considering legislation banning them right now.

00:41:40.150 --> 00:41:51.350
 So do all pesticides affect bees the same ways? And what, once again, like in your imagination,

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:56.680
 what would it look like to have a field that a conventional farmer did spray sometimes?

00:41:56.680 --> 00:42:02.400
 And what would be the ideal conditions to do that? Or how, how does that work? Like, is it an absolute?

00:42:03.690 --> 00:42:10.550
 Yeah. So some pesticides are not rated as toxic to bees. They actually don't have harmful effects.

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:16.470
 And unfortunately, this isn't totally my expertise. So I can't name all of the compounds, but there's

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:23.680
 some great resources out there. Oregon State University has a publication called How to Avoid

00:42:23.680 --> 00:42:30.320
 Bee Poisonings from Pesticides that has a really comprehensive list. And they flag them red, yellow,

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:35.840
 or green. Green being the ones that you can spray and bees shouldn't really be harmed versus red

00:42:35.840 --> 00:42:40.720
 would be the ones to avoid at all costs. The yellow ones are more interesting. They might have,

00:42:40.720 --> 00:42:47.290
 say, be very toxic for a short period, but have a short residual toxicity so they would

00:42:47.290 --> 00:42:52.130
 dissipate from the environment quickly. So for those, you might be able to spray them at night

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:58.540
 when bees aren't active. And then, you know, given the right conditions, they wouldn't be available

00:42:58.540 --> 00:43:05.740
 the next day and not that many bees would be harmed. So often for producers, we ask them to think about,

00:43:05.740 --> 00:43:12.360
 you know, what the risk is to bees to look at that and then decide when to spray. Maybe don't spray during

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:18.120
 bloom, spray after bloom when bees aren't present. There are some chemicals when you mix them,

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:23.400
 they become more toxic. So avoiding those mixtures, spraying at night, and of course,

00:43:23.400 --> 00:43:28.920
 always calibrating their spray equipment and spraying during appropriate conditions where there's not a high

00:43:28.920 --> 00:43:35.480
 chance of drift or inversion that could carry the pesticides miles away. So those are some of the

00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:42.040
 things we recommend. And I work for a great nonprofit right now. And we go through, you know, a pesticide

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:48.680
 report with a farmer, suggest alternatives, suggest when they should spray, and also work with them on

00:43:48.680 --> 00:43:54.400
 non-chemical mechanisms. So some farmers go through and they'll vacuum a crop. This happens a lot in

00:43:54.400 --> 00:44:00.720
 strawberry. So it's a physical means of getting rid of a pest. There are pheromone traps that are insect

00:44:00.720 --> 00:44:07.400
 specific, so only the males will go to that trap and you can reduce their populations. But there's also

00:44:07.400 --> 00:44:16.230
 conservation biocontrol. Basically, often you plant the same habitats that support pollinators, support

00:44:16.230 --> 00:44:23.860
 beneficial insects, ladybugs, right? Lacewings. These are predaceous ground beetles. They'll go to these

00:44:23.860 --> 00:44:29.540
 habitats and they go out and do natural pest control. Predaceous ground beetles will go out and kill slugs,

00:44:29.540 --> 00:44:35.380
 and they're actually so aggressive. They'll kill more prey than they can even eat, which is a farmer's

00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:43.860
 dream, right? So we try and promote this suite of methods whereby farmers can reduce the need to apply

00:44:43.860 --> 00:44:49.700
 pesticides. And then when they are applying it, do it in a much smarter way that doesn't harm the insects

00:44:49.700 --> 00:44:51.220
 that they want to promote on their farm.

00:44:51.220 --> 00:45:02.030
 And obviously has less environmental impacts or there's less risk of other things going wrong,

00:45:02.030 --> 00:45:08.090
 too, right? With less pesticide use all around. Is this the program that is the pollinator

00:45:08.090 --> 00:45:10.490
 conservation program? Is that the same thing?

